module bindings.swift.streaming_decompression_completion_unchecked;

// A streaming decompressor hands the peer control of the expansion ratio:
// the transport bounds only the compressed bytes, while the decompressed
// size is whatever the stream says. The SwiftNIO decompressors are driven by
// a loop bounded only by the compressed body's remaining byte count
// (`while part.readableBytes > 0`), and the zlib wrapper underneath
// consumes inflate's return code only as an error-code guard, discarding
// the completion bit -- so a peer appending data behind the compressed
// stream keeps the driver inflating past the end of the message (the
// spin/zip-bomb vector), and a truncated stream ends unnoticed. The finding
// is the Swift member of the streaming decompression family whose Python,
// Rust and Go bindings live in this repository; each member discriminates on
// its ecosystem's documented bounding vocabulary -- max_length and
// unconsumed_tail for Python's decompressor steps, min/clamp/take/truncate
// for Rust's output pipeline, io.LimitReader for Go's reader-drain loops.
// Swift's is the completion protocol: a driver that consults a completion
// state names it, in any of the usual spellings (complete, finished, done,
// either capitalisation), and any such identifier in the file suppresses,
// because the finding is about the driver ignoring completion rather than
// about which construct consults it.
//
// The anchor is necessarily file-wide: swift.go emits exactly one context
// node per file and no per-function facts, and while-statements lower to a
// conditionless nir.Loop, so "a decompression step driven by the compressed
// remainder" is approximated by the file containing both the step and the
// ByteBuffer remainder read that bounds the loop (readableBytes). The step
// is matched exactly -- the decompressor's decompress and zlib's inflate,
// the two spellings the API documents, the same exact-match choice the
// Python member makes -- so constructing a decompressor
// (NIOHTTPRequestDecompressor(limit:)) or naming one in prose does not
// reach, and comments produce no AST nodes either. Residuals: a step spelled
// differently (gunzip, a bespoke inflateStream) does not anchor; a
// completion state spelled outside the family (atEnd, exhausted) does not
// suppress; zlib's Z_STREAM_END error-code guard deliberately does not
// count, because the wrapper's guard is present whether or not the
// completion bit is propagated; and an unrelated completion-spelled
// identifier anywhere in the file suppresses -- the false-negative
// direction, the same residual the Python member documents for
// positionally-bounded steps.
binding streamingDecompressionCompletionUnchecked {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "module" and node.context.language == "swift" and node.context.call in ["decompress", "inflate"] and containsAny(node.context.call, ["readableBytes"]) and not (containsAny(node.context.identifier, ["complet", "finish", "done"]))
  emit issue code.UnboundedResourceOperation at node
}
